##
    Functions as values.

    A function is a value like any other: it has a type, it can be stored in a
    variable, held in a set, passed to another function, and handed back from one. Everything
    below is that one idea in different shapes.

    A function's type is written the way its declaration is: the result, then 'function', then
    what it takes. So 'integer delegate(integer)' takes an integer and yields one, and
    'delegate(string)' takes a string and yields nothing.

    Two words, two jobs: 'function' declares one or makes one on the spot, and 'delegate'
    writes the type of one. That is why 'integer delegate(integer) function AdderOf(...)'
    below reads without doubling back — the first word says what comes out, the second says
    that a function is being declared.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.WritingThemDown();
        Program.WhereTheTypesGo();
        Program.PassingThemAlong();
        Program.HandingThemBack();
        Program.OneAtATime();
        Program.Remembering();
        Program.NamingOneThatAlreadyExists();
        Program.HoldingAnyFunctionAtAll();
    end function

    # ---- The two ways to write one ------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A short function is written on one line: its parameters, then 'yield', then the one
        thing it produces. A longer one is written out with 'function' and closed with
        'end function', and yields its result the way any other function does.

        Both spell producing a value the same way, because it is the same act. Nothing new has
        to be learned to read the short form.
    ##
    function WritingThemDown()
        integer delegate(integer) increment = (a) yield a + 1;

        integer delegate(integer, integer) larger = function(a, b)
            if a > b
                yield a;
            else
                yield b;
            end if
        end function;

        # One that takes nothing, and one that yields nothing.
        string delegate() greet = () yield "hello";
        delegate(string) announce = (what) yield Console.WriteLine("* " + what);

        announce(greet() + ", " + increment(41) + ", " + larger(3, 9));
    end function

    # ---- Where the types go -------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A parameter is a bare name wherever the surrounding code already says what it holds.
        Above, 'integer delegate(integer)' on the left says 'a' is an integer, so writing it
        again on the right would say the same thing twice — and the compiler says so.

        Four things say it, and between them they cover every place a lambda can be written:
        a declared type, the element type of a set being built, the parameter of the function
        being called, and the result of the function doing the yielding.

        That leaves exactly one place a lambda writes its own types, and it is the place
        nothing else says anything: a 'let'. The two rules meet with no gap between them, so
        a lambda always has one spelling that says nothing twice and leaves nothing unsaid.
    ##
    function WhereTheTypesGo()
        # Nothing on the left, so the lambda says it.
        let halve = (integer n) yield n / 2;

        # The declared type says it, so the lambda does not.
        integer delegate(integer) double = (n) yield n * 2;

        # The element type says it.
        integer delegate(integer)[] steps = { (n) yield n + 1, (n) yield n - 1 };

        Console.WriteLine("halved:  " + halve(10));
        Console.WriteLine("doubled: " + double(10));
        Console.WriteLine("stepped: " + steps[0](10) + " " + steps[1](10));
    end function

    # ---- Taking one as a parameter -------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A function that takes a function is the whole point of them being values. Apply does
        not know what it is applying, which is what lets one function serve every caller.
    ##
    integer[] function Apply(integer[] values, integer delegate(integer) transform)
        integer[] results = {};

        for each value in values
            results.Insert(transform(value));
        end for

        yield results;
    end function

    # @summary: Handing a function to another function as an argument.
    function PassingThemAlong()
        integer[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4};

        # Apply's own parameter says what 'n' holds.
        Console.WriteLine("doubled: " + Program.Apply(numbers, (n) yield n * 2));
        Console.WriteLine("squared: " + Program.Apply(numbers, (n) yield n * n));

        # A block-bodied one is written the same way wherever it goes.
        Console.WriteLine("clamped: " + Program.Apply(numbers, function(n)
            if n > 3
                yield 3;
            end if

            yield n;
        end function));

        # A set of them, since a function is a value and a set holds values.
        integer delegate(integer)[] steps =
        {
            (n) yield n + 10,
            (n) yield n * 10
        };

        for each operation in steps
            Console.WriteLine("step of 7: " + operation(7));
        end for
    end function

    # ---- Handing one back ---------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A function may yield a function. The one handed back remembers 'by', which belonged to
        the call that made it — that is what closing over a value means. The declared result
        says what 'n' holds, so the lambda being yielded leaves it out.
    ##
    integer delegate(integer) function AdderOf(integer by)
        yield (n) yield n + by;
    end function

    # @summary: A function that yields another function.
    function HandingThemBack()
        integer delegate(integer) addFive = Program.AdderOf(5);
        integer delegate(integer) addHundred = Program.AdderOf(100);

        Console.WriteLine("add five to 1:    " + addFive(1));
        Console.WriteLine("add hundred to 1: " + addHundred(1));

        # One made and called on the spot, which needs no name at all.
        Console.WriteLine("twice of 6:       " + Program.AdderOf(6)(6));

        ##
            AdderOf's own type, written out. It takes an integer and yields
            'integer delegate(integer)', so the whole thing is that with another
            'delegate(integer)' around it — a result may be a function type like any other,
            and 'delegate' says so as many times as it needs to.

            Only 'delegate' can appear after a result, so a reader and a parser both know that
            each one begins another type rather than a declaration. That is what lets these
            nest at all.
        ##
        integer delegate(integer) delegate(integer) maker = Program.AdderOf;

        Console.WriteLine("through maker:    " + maker(5)(3));

        # Held without its shape. Function is the root of every one of them, so anything goes
        # in — but nothing comes out callable, since calling needs a shape.
        Function shapeless = maker;

        Console.WriteLine("still the same:   " + Reference.Equals(shapeless, maker));
    end function

    # ---- One number at a time -------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        Three numbers added, taken one at a time. Each call hands back something that is still
        waiting for the next, so the nesting goes as deep as there are numbers:

            integer                                                    what comes out
            integer delegate(integer)                                  after two
            integer delegate(integer) delegate(integer)                after one
            integer delegate(integer) delegate(integer) delegate(integer)   SumOf itself

        Only the declared result is written. The lambdas inside say nothing about what they
        hold, because the type on the outside already did — it says what 'b' is, and what the
        thing yielded from it must be, which in turn says what 'c' is.
    ##
    integer delegate(integer) delegate(integer) function SumOf(integer a)
        yield (b) yield (c) yield a + b + c;
    end function

    # @summary: A set of functions, called in turn.
    function OneAtATime()
        Console.WriteLine("1 + 2 + 3:        " + Program.SumOf(1)(2)(3));

        # Stopping part way keeps the rest waiting, and what is waiting can be kept and
        # used more than once.
        integer delegate(integer) delegate(integer) fromTen = Program.SumOf(10);
        integer delegate(integer) tenAndTwenty = fromTen(20);

        Console.WriteLine("10 + 20 + 30:     " + tenAndTwenty(30));
        Console.WriteLine("10 + 20 + 70:     " + tenAndTwenty(70));
        Console.WriteLine("10 + 5 + 5:       " + fromTen(5)(5));
    end function

    # ---- What a function remembers -------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A loop variable is fresh on every turn, so each function made inside the loop closes
        over its own. This is the trap that catches people in languages where the variable is
        shared: there, every function would report the last value.
    ##
    function Remembering()
        integer delegate()[] reporters = {};

        for i = 1 to 3
            reporters.Insert(() yield i);
        end for

        for each reporter in reporters
            Console.Write(reporter() + " ");
        end for

        Console.WriteLine();
    end function

    # ---- Naming one that already exists --------------------------------------------

    # @summary: Multiplies by three, declared so it can be passed as a value.
    # @n: the number to triple.
    # @yields: three times it.
    integer function Triple(integer n)
        yield n * 3;
    end function

    ##
        A function that has a name is already a value, so it needs no lambda around it. A
        member reached through an instance is that member bound to the instance, and calling it
        later still knows which one it belongs to.
    ##
    function NamingOneThatAlreadyExists()
        integer delegate(integer) tripled = Program.Triple;

        Console.WriteLine("tripled: " + Program.Apply({1, 2, 3}, tripled));

        Counter counter = new Counter(10);
        integer delegate() advance = counter.Next;

        Console.WriteLine("counting: " + advance() + " " + advance() + " " + counter.Next());
    end function

    # ---- Holding one without naming its shape ---------------------------------------

    ##
        'Function' is the type every function type descends from, so it holds one whatever it
        takes and yields. A set holds one type, which is why a root is needed at all: without
        it there is no way to keep functions of different shapes together.

        It says nothing about what the parameters hold, so a lambda written into one writes its
        own types — the same rule as everywhere else, reaching the same answer. And it cannot be
        called, because calling needs a signature and this is the type that has none.
    ##
    function HoldingAnyFunctionAtAll()
        Function[] assorted =
        {
            (integer n) yield n + 1,
            Program.Triple,
            (string what) yield Console.WriteLine("* " + what)
        };

        Console.WriteLine("held: " + assorted.Count());

        # Every reference type is a Model, and a Function is a reference type.
        Model asModel = assorted[0];
        Console.WriteLine("still the same one: " + Reference.Equals(asModel, assorted[0]));
    end function

end model

# @summary: A number that goes up, for showing what a captured reference sees.
model Counter

    # @summary: How many times it has been raised.
    integer count;

    # @summary: A counter beginning at the given number.
    # @start: what to count up from.
    public function Counter(integer start)
        this.count = start;
    end function

    # @summary: Raises the count by one and hands back the new value.
    # @yields: the count after raising it.
    public integer function Next()
        this.count = this.count + 1;
        yield this.count;
    end function

end model
